# Tesserafin Server @VERSION@ — portable Linux archive (@RID@)

This archive is the *portable* distribution: the same application and web payload
as the `.deb` and `.rpm` packages, unpacked wherever you want it.

**It installs nothing.** It creates no user, registers no systemd service and
writes nothing outside the directories you give it on the command line. If you
want a managed service, install the `.deb` or the `.rpm` instead.

## Layout

```
bin/tesserafin                       symlink into lib/tesserafin/tesserafin
lib/tesserafin/                      the server application (self-contained .NET build)
lib/tesserafin/ffmpeg/bin/ffmpeg     the bundled Tesserafin FFmpeg runtime
lib/tesserafin/ffmpeg/bin/ffprobe
lib/tesserafin/ffmpeg/lib/           the runtime's own shared libraries
share/tesserafin/web/                the bundled Tesserafin Web client
share/tesserafin/web-revision.json   provenance of that web payload
share/tesserafin/ffmpeg/             the runtime's SBOM, source manifest,
                                     compiled-capability record and notices
LICENSE                              the server licence, GPL-2.0-or-later
LICENSES.md                          the licence index for the whole archive
licenses/ffmpeg/                     the runtime's per-component licence texts
FFMPEG-CORRESPONDING-SOURCE.txt      where to get the runtime's complete source
```

`bin/` and `lib/` inside `lib/tesserafin/ffmpeg/` are **siblings on purpose**.
The executables carry `RUNPATH=$ORIGIN/../lib` and load their bundled libraries
relative to themselves, which is what makes this archive relocatable: unpack it
anywhere and the encoder still resolves its own libraries and never a system
copy. Moving `bin/ffmpeg` out of that directory breaks it.

## Requirements

The application payload carries its own .NET runtime, so no system .NET is
needed. It does need a few ordinary system libraries:

* `libicu` (globalization is on; the server refuses to start without it)
* `fontconfig` and a font package such as DejaVu (image and subtitle rendering)
* `libssl` / `openssl-libs`
* `glibc` 2.28 or newer

## Running it

Pick your own directories. Nothing is created for you and nothing defaults to a
system path.

```sh
PREFIX="$PWD"
./bin/tesserafin \
    --configdir "$PREFIX/state/config" \
    --datadir   "$PREFIX/state/data" \
    --cachedir  "$PREFIX/state/cache" \
    --logdir    "$PREFIX/state/log" \
    --webdir    "$PREFIX/share/tesserafin/web" \
    --ffmpeg    "$PREFIX/lib/tesserafin/ffmpeg/bin/ffmpeg"
```

The server listens on port 8096. `http://127.0.0.1:8096/` redirects to `/web/`
and serves the first-run wizard.

`--ffmpeg` is not optional in practice: the server validates the encoder at
startup and refuses to start without a usable one. Always point it at the
bundled binary rather than letting it search `$PATH`, so you get the encoder this
build was tested against instead of whatever your distribution ships.

Run it as an unprivileged user. Nothing here needs root.

## Provenance

| Input | Value | Licence |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Server commit | `@VCS_REF@` | GPL-2.0-or-later |
| Tesserafin Web commit | `@WEB_VCS_REF@` | see `share/tesserafin/web-licenses/` |
| FFmpeg runtime revision | `@F0_BUILD_REVISION@` | GPL-3.0-or-later |
| FFmpeg upstream commit | `@F0_UPSTREAM_COMMIT@` | |

The server and the bundled FFmpeg runtime are **separately licensed works**. The
server is GPL-2.0-or-later and is not relicensed by being distributed alongside a
GPL-3.0-or-later runtime. `LICENSES.md` states the boundary and points at every
licence text in this archive.

`share/tesserafin/web-revision.json` records the web payload's own provenance as
the web build produced it. `share/tesserafin/ffmpeg/SOURCE.json` and
`share/tesserafin/ffmpeg/sbom.cdx.json` do the same for the runtime, component by
component, and `FFMPEG-CORRESPONDING-SOURCE.txt` names the corresponding-source
archive distributed alongside this one.

## Compiled capability is not tested capability

`share/tesserafin/ffmpeg/capability.json` lists what the runtime was **compiled**
with. Listing an encoder proves it was compiled in and nothing more. What has
actually been executed, and what has not, is recorded in
`docs/distribution/L0-linux-packages.md` in the server repository.
